## Goal

Make BaseHarbor state safely evolvable after the v0.5.0 contract freeze without creating migration ceremony for normal users.

> **Developer experience first.**
>
> **As little as possible, as much as necessary.**

A supported upgrade should normally remain:

```text
baha up
  -> detect state version
  -> preflight migration
  -> migrate if safe
  -> verify
  -> continue
```

## Scope

Target semantics themselves are not defined by this issue. **#408 / v0.4.15** already owns Target, Access, Scope, Target-scoped state and deployment identity. This issue only versions/migrates those persisted structures as an existing compatibility surface.

Define and implement compatibility/version handling for BaseHarbor-owned state where applicable:

- deployment/operator state;
- runtime state;
- capability-provider state;
- secure bindings and ownership metadata;
- generated/protected state;
- backup/restore metadata;
- audit/evidence state that requires durable compatibility.

Requirements:

- explicit state/schema version ownership;
- deterministic migrations;
- migration preflight before mutation;
- safe handling of partially failed migrations;
- unknown incompatible future state fails closed;
- existing authoritative ownership/isolation invariants remain preserved;
- no runtime/provider implementation detail is promoted into portable application intent.

## Migration rules

- migrate only state BaseHarbor owns;
- prefer additive migration where practical;
- never silently discard unknown or security-relevant state;
- verify authoritative invariants after migration;
- migration failure must leave an actionable recovery path;
- rollback is required only where technically safe and meaningful; recovery may use the last verified state/backup instead.

## Developer experience

- normal supported upgrades should be automatic;
- warnings/errors must explain what state needs migration and why;
- no manual migration file editing in the normal path;
- no global migration framework unless current state actually requires it;
- keep migrations small, explicit and testable.

## Explicitly out of scope

- Kubernetes state/resources;
- OpenShift state;
- cloud-provider implementation state;
- HA/topology semantics;
- distributed control-plane migrations;
- rolling multi-node upgrades.

## Deployment/runtime state compatibility

State versioning must explicitly cover the runtime-neutral deployment boundary that later runtimes depend on.

Where persisted, version/migration ownership must be defined for:

- deployment target identity;
- selected runtime/provider identity;
- runtime access-source reference metadata, excluding credential material;
- platform-resource reference metadata;
- ownership classification of external/platform-managed resources;
- capability/permission observations that affect planning;
- typed unsupported/unverifiable/authorization/conflict state where durable state is actually needed.

Rules:

- never migrate credentials into portable application state;
- never convert platform-owned resources into BaseHarbor-owned resources implicitly;
- runtime-specific realization state stays behind the runtime boundary;
- migrations must preserve the distinction between portable intent, deployment/operator state and runtime/provider state.


## Acceptance

- [ ] Migrations preserve external/platform ownership and never import credential material into portable application state.

- [ ] Deployment target/access/platform-reference state has explicit ownership/versioning where persisted.

- [ ] Supported v0.4/v0.5 state has explicit version ownership.
- [ ] Supported old state can be migrated deterministically.
- [ ] Migration preflight is non-mutating.
- [ ] Failed/incompatible migration does not continue into unsafe apply.
- [ ] Ownership, isolation and secret-safety invariants survive migration.
- [ ] Post-migration verification checks the authoritative behavior/state.
- [ ] Normal supported migration is low-friction for developers.
- [ ] Tests cover success, invalid state, interrupted/failed migration and unsupported future state.
- [ ] Documentation follows `docs/DEVELOPMENT_GUIDELINES.md`.
- [ ] No unnecessary GitHub Actions are introduced or triggered.

Depends on: #175
Parent roadmap: #155


## Pre-release gate

Before creating this release:

- [ ] All acceptance criteria in this issue are complete.
- [ ] Required local/repository tests pass.
- [ ] The full relevant CI matrix has passed at least once on the **exact release-candidate commit**.
- [ ] Docker and Podman acceptance pass where applicable.
- [ ] Security, isolation and secret-leak checks pass where applicable.
- [ ] The documentation audit defined by #364 passes.
- [ ] Human-facing documentation matches the shipped behavior.
- [ ] Reference and normative specs match every changed public contract.
- [ ] No unresolved release blocker remains.
- [ ] Version, changelog, release notes and release metadata are consistent.
- [ ] Final exact-head acceptance evidence is recorded before tagging/releasing.

Acceptance answers whether the feature/release scope is complete. This gate answers whether the exact commit is safe and documented well enough to release.

Do not weaken or skip a required check merely to complete the release. Checks that are genuinely not applicable should be recorded as such rather than silently omitted.


## Provider Catalog selection and state migration

State versioning must preserve the distinction between catalog discovery metadata and instantiated provider/runtime state.

Where BaseHarbor persists provider selection/resolution, define explicit version ownership for:

- selected provider family;
- stable provider ID;
- resolved provider implementation version;
- Provider Protocol compatibility metadata;
- resolved OCI artifact reference and authoritative digest where persisted;
- selected service/capability contract version where applicable;
- selected Delivery Provider identity and reconciliation ownership where persisted.

Migration rules:

- catalog metadata is not copied into portable application intent;
- provider catalog entries are not treated as runtime instances;
- existing `provider-registry.json` instance/binding/ownership semantics remain runtime state;
- old provider/runtime identifiers migrate deterministically to the frozen provider identity model where needed;
- immutable artifact identity uses digest when available;
- a missing or incompatible catalog/provider identity fails preflight before mutation.

### Provider catalog/state acceptance

- [ ] Persisted provider resolution has explicit state ownership and versioning where needed.
- [ ] Provider Catalog identity and Provider Runtime Registry instance identity remain distinct after migration.
- [ ] OCI artifact digest identity survives supported migrations without silent substitution.
- [ ] Runtime, Capability and Delivery Provider selections can evolve independently without entering portable application intent.

